A basic agent loop gives the model a goal and available tools, receives a proposed action, executes that action, feeds back the environment result, and repeats until the task is complete or the runtime stops it. The model supplies decisions; the runtime owns execution. Put hard limits around iterations, elapsed time, tokens, tool calls, spend, and failure retries. Add cancellation, timeouts, idempotency, tracing, and a clear terminal state. For consequential actions, pause for user confirmation or route to a human. A loop without budgets and observability is an uncontrolled distributed workflow, not a production agent.

```ts caption="The runtime, not the model, owns the stop condition."
for (let step = 0; step < MAX_STEPS; step++) {
  const turn = await model.run(state);
  if (turn.final) return turn.answer;
  const result = await executeValidatedTool(turn.toolCall);
  state = appendObservation(state, result);
}
throw new Error('agent step budget exceeded');
```

The application sends the model the conversation and a small set of tool definitions. The model may return one or more tool calls with a name and arguments; it does not execute your code. The runtime must identify the allowed function, parse and validate the arguments, apply authorization and business rules, execute the operation, and return a tool result associated with the call ID. The application then sends the preserved model output plus those results back for a final response or more calls. Treat every call as untrusted input, make side effects idempotent where possible, and enforce timeouts, budgets, and audit logging.

```ts caption="Validate and authorize before executing a model-proposed tool call."
for (const call of response.toolCalls) {
  const args = toolSchema.parse(JSON.parse(call.arguments));
  authorize(user, call.name, args);
  const result = await tools[call.name](args);
  messages.push({ role: 'tool', tool_call_id: call.id, content: JSON.stringify(result) });
}
```
